coupon/governance mechanism - rather than the repository's current default
branch, which by 2026 reflects ESD's final "EIP-25: Enable Migration"
state (a one-time, hardcoded wind-down contract ESD used to migrate its
V1 DAO off-chain into a V2 system, not a deployable protocol).

Modifications made to the original source, as required by Apache License
2.0 Section 4(b):
  - Renamed the protocol and token from "Empty Set Dollar" / "ESD" to
    "Liquid Supply Dollar" / "LSD" (after £sd - librae, solidi,
    denarii, the historical English money of account), and the
    bonded/stake token from "Empty Set Dollar Stake" / "ESDS" to
    "Liquid Supply Dollar Stake" / "LSDS", throughout contracts, tests,
    and configuration.
  - Renamed package identifiers and deployment environment variables
    accordingly.
  - Constants.sol: CHAIN_ID set to Robinhood Chain Mainnet (4663).
  - Constants.sol: retimed around 6-hour epochs (EPOCH_PERIOD 21600, four a
    day) instead of ESD's daily ones. Several constants there count in
    epochs rather than in time, so they were rescaled together to preserve
    ESD's wall-clock windows: COUPON_EXPIRATION 90 -> 360 (still 90 days),
    GOVERNANCE_PERIOD 7 -> 28 (still 7 days), and the exit lockup from
    upstream's hardcoded single 24-hour epoch to DAO_EXIT_LOCKUP_EPOCHS = 16
    (4 days at six-hour epochs). The pool's is shorter,
    POOL_EXIT_LOCKUP_EPOCHS = 12 (3 days): providing liquidity already
    carries impermanent loss that DAO bonding does not, and the oracle reads
    its price from a pool, so that is the last place to add friction.
    Upstream hardcoded both to one epoch; they are constants now.
    BOOTSTRAPPING_PERIOD counts forced +10%
    expansions rather than time, so the phase multiplies supply by
    1.1^BOOTSTRAPPING_PERIOD. It is 45 here (~73x over 3.75 days at 2h
    bootstrap epochs) against ESD's 90 (~5,300x): genesis bond capital is
    divided by whatever multiplication follows it, and this fork wants the
    genesis floor to survive its own genesis.
  - Constants.sol: ORACLE_POOL_RATIO raised from 5 to 25, so a quarter of
    each expansion goes to liquidity providers rather than a twentieth.
    ESD's 5% left the pool thin; DSD, forking ESD after watching it run,
    went to 35%. The oracle only reports a usable price while the pool has
    liquidity, so what LPs are paid is not a side reward - it is what keeps
    the price feed alive. Side effect worth knowing: the bonded share of
    total supply now converges to 75% during sustained expansion rather
    than 95%, since the pool's cut compounds outside the DAO. The deploy
    script reads the epoch length back off the chain and refuses to finish
    if it does not match what the operator expected, since switching only
    some of these values is the failure mode.
  - Added Robinhood Chain network configuration to the build and
    deploy tooling.

Price oracle and LP-incentive pool: moved from Uniswap V2 to Uniswap V4
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Upstream ESD's `Oracle.sol` and `Pool.sol` hardcode Ethereum mainnet's
Uniswap V2 factory address and read/provide liquidity through a V2 pair.
On Robinhood Chain, Uniswap v2/v3/v4 and the UniversalRouter are
officially deployed, so the deploy uses the official PoolManager rather
than bringing one. Note that the UniversalRouter there is a fork whose v4
swap struct carries an extra `minHopPriceX36` field, so stock Uniswap SDK
calldata reverts against it; the frontend encodes the struct by hand to
match. Given that, this fork targets
Uniswap V4 rather than re-deploying V2, and - importantly - keeps the
price-reading pool and the LP-incentivized liquidity in the exact same V4
pool. An earlier draft of this fork considered reading price from a
separate, dedicated V4 pool while keeping LP incentives on a V2 pair; that
would have made the price trivially manipulable (a thinly-traded pricing
pool, unconnected to where the real liquidity/trading actually happens),
so it was rejected in favor of the single-pool design below.

  - `protocol/v4/src/Watchtower.sol` (new file, Solidity ^0.8.24, not a
    modification of any upstream file): a Uniswap V4 hook that manually
    accumulates a TWAP price (afterInitialize/afterSwap), exposing a
    `capture()` with the same shape as upstream `Oracle.capture()`
    (price, valid). "Valid" is approximated using the pool's current
    in-range liquidity rather than upstream's x*y=k reserve check, since
    V4 concentrated liquidity has no single "reserve" the way V2 does -
    not a 1:1 semantic port, only an analogous safety check with the same
    intent.
  - `protocol/v4/src/Quiver.sol` (new file, Solidity ^0.8.24, replaces
    `Pool.sol`/`Liquidity.sol`/`PoolState.sol`/`PoolGetters.sol`/
    `PoolSetters.sol`, all removed): V4 has no fungible LP-token analogous
    to a V2 pair's ERC20 share token, so this contract itself becomes the
    pool's sole liquidity position (one fixed tick range) and tracks each
    user's proportional share internally as "liquidity units" in place of
    upstream's `univ2` ERC20 balance. The staged/bonded/phantom/claimable
    bonding and reward-distribution accounting (including the
    Frozen/Fluid anti-manipulation window on bond()/unbond()) is otherwise
    byte-for-byte the same logic as upstream `Pool.sol` - it never
    actually depended on `univ2` being an ERC20, just a fungible unit.
  - `protocol/contracts/oracle/Longbow.sol`: rewritten to call
    `Watchtower.capture()` through a local, 0.5.x-declared minimal
    interface (`IV4OracleHook`) instead of reading a V2 pair directly.
    Solidity can call any already-deployed contract through an
    ABI-compatible interface regardless of which compiler version
    produced it (this is exactly how upstream's Oracle.sol already called
    the externally-deployed Uniswap V2 Factory/Pair) - it just cannot
    `new` a contract across a pragma-major-version boundary, which is why
    the hook and pool live in `protocol/v4/` as separate ^0.8.24
    artifacts deployed by the deploy script rather than by Bowyer/Stringer
    themselves.
  - `protocol/contracts/oracle/IOracle.sol`: `setup()` removed (pool
    initialization now happens once, directly, as part of the deploy
    script rather than being triggered by the DAO);
    `pair()` renamed to `hook()`.
  - `protocol/contracts/dao/Deployer.sol`: `Bowyer`/`Stringer` no
    longer construct Oracle/Pool's AMM dependencies themselves. Both now
    read a compile-time placeholder address (`HOOK_ADDRESS`,
    `POOL_ADDRESS`, both `address(0)` until filled in by the deploy
    script - same pattern as the removed `UNISWAP_FACTORY`/`USDC`
    placeholders) for the already-deployed `Watchtower`/`Quiver`.
    `Bowyer.initialize()` also calls the hook's one-time `setDao()` to
    hand control from the deployer (temporary "controller" since
    deployment) to the newly-created Oracle - this breaks the circular
    dependency between the hook (needs Oracle's address) and Oracle
    (needs the hook's address), since both are unknown until the other
    exists; see `Watchtower.sol`'s `controller`/`setDao()` docs.
  - Test scaffolding updated to match the `IOracle` interface change
    (no `setup()`, `hook()` instead of `pair()`).
  - Removed as no longer reachable from the deploy path: `Pool.sol`,
    `Liquidity.sol`, `PoolState.sol`, `PoolGetters.sol`, `PoolSetters.sol`,
    `MockPool.sol`, `MockPoolState.sol`, `MockOracle.sol`,
    `MockUniswapV2PairTrade.sol`, `MockUniswapV2PairLiquidity.sol`,
    `external/UniswapV2Library.sol`, `external/UniswapV2OracleLibrary.sol`,
    and their corresponding `test/oracle/Pool.test.js`,
    `test/oracle/PoolState.test.js`, `test/oracle/Oracle.test.js` (their
    coverage is superseded by `protocol/v4/test/*.t.sol`, which test
    against a real, locally-simulated V4 PoolManager rather than mocks).
  - `@uniswap/v2-core`/`@uniswap/lib` dependencies removed (no longer
    used by the 0.5.x side).

Addition: bond-backed treasury and a redemption floor
------------------------------------------------------
`protocol/contracts/dao/Treasury.sol` (new file, not a modification of any
upstream ESD source), inherited by `Greenwood` alongside Bonding,
Market, Regulator and Govern.

Upstream ESD had one answer to a price below a dollar: coupons, which only
pay out if the supply expands again. That makes them a bet on recovery
rather than a floor, and when the recovery did not come there was nothing
underneath. This adds two halves that only work together:

  - `purchaseBond(asset, amount)` takes a listed reserve asset now and owes
    LSD at a discount after BOND_VESTING_EPOCHS. The asset stays with the
    protocol. That window is counted in epochs but aimed at wall-clock:
    bootstrap epochs run at 1/BOOTSTRAPPING_SPEEDUP_FACTOR of the normal
    length, so `Getters.bondVestingEpochs()` scales the count up during
    bootstrapping and tapers it across the boundary, where part of the wait
    is served by short epochs and the rest by full-length ones. A flat count
    would hold the number and lose the duration - twelve epochs bought in the
    last genesis epoch would run nearly three days rather than one. LSD is minted at claim rather than at
    purchase, so nothing waits inside the contract and the supply the
    oracle sees is not inflated by promises not yet due. Payouts route
    through a new `Comptroller.mintToBondHolder`, which deliberately
    creates no debt - the supply is backed rather than conjured, and
    routing it through `mintToAccount` would have the protocol believe it
    was in deficit and open coupon sales it has no business opening.
  - `redeem(dollarAmount)` burns LSD and pays out that LSD's pro-rata share
    of everything the protocol holds. This is what makes the backing real:
    without it "backing" is a dashboard number; with it, LSD cannot trade
    far below backing for long. It is unconditional on purpose - gating it
    on a contraction would make it a promise rather than a mechanism.

Redemption is exactly pro-rata and so cannot break itself: burning `d` of a
supply `S` against a treasury `T` pays `T*d/S` and leaves `T/S` behind, so a
rush shrinks both sides together and every remaining holder is left as
backed as before. Integer division rounds the payout down, so the floor can
only tick up.

Bonds are credited on the balance delta the transfer actually produced, not
on the amount the caller named - a token that skims a transfer fee, or
rounds internally, cannot be owed LSD for value the treasury never
received. The backing the price clamp compares against is read before that
transfer, so a bonder's own deposit is not counted against them.

Four guards, all covered by `protocol/v4/test/Treasury.t.sol` (25 tests
against the real compiled 0.5.17 bytecode):
  - A bond can never price below current backing: the effective price
    clamps to backing rather than refusing (the same max(price, book) form
    NetNet Capital quotes bonds with). A bond at backing grows treasury and
    supply in proportion - integer rounding makes it weakly accretive - so
    no price exists at which bonding lowers the floor, and no market
    condition in which the discount closes the window.
  - Issuance is capped per epoch at BOND_SUPPLY_LIMIT (10% of supply), so no
    single actor can mint an unbounded amount of discounted supply. The cap
    is deliberately generous: from a standing start a percent an epoch would
    take months to build any backing worth having, and the guard that
    actually protects holders is the one above.
  - Bonds are closed whenever the oracle reported its pool too thin -
    `Regulator.oracleCapture()` records the price bonds may sell against,
    zero when there is none. During bootstrapping it records the pinned
    flat GENESIS_BOND_PRICE (5.00), undiscounted, with no market input at
    all - the oracle's validity check is only "the pool holds liquidity",
    so at genesis dust listed at any price would read as a market, high to
    shut the door or low to open it cheap. A price that reads nothing
    cannot be fed anything. Five dollars for a token targeting one is paid
    for the multiplication still ahead of early supply, which also quiets
    the door by itself late in the bootstrap. During bootstrapping the
    per-epoch capacity is GENESIS_BOND_SUPPLY_LIMIT's 20% rather than the
    normal 10% - wider because at the flat genesis price every sale raises
    backing and there is no manipulable market to defend against, and still
    capped because an open door would let early capital corner the genesis
    multiplication everyone later is diluted against. Expansion itself
    still runs on the pinned 1.10.
  - Bonding stops protocol-wide whenever any single reserve cannot be
    priced. An unpriceable asset drops out of the treasury sum, which
    understates backing, which makes the guard above easier to clear rather
    than harder - so the sum failing has to close bonding, not relax it.

Redemption also clamps `totalDebt` to the remaining supply. Deep in a
contraction, debt raised against a larger supply can outlast it, and
Comptroller asserts debt <= supply - without the clamp the floor would seize
exactly when it is needed.

What this does not do is make the floor rise by itself. Every expansion
mints LSD against no new counter tokens and thins the backing, so bonds and
swap fees have to bring in more than expansions dilute. And a bonder who
sells the moment their bond vests is selling into the same market the price
depends on; the discount buys their patience for the vesting window and no
longer.

A multi-asset reserve
---------------------
The treasury is not limited to the counter token. `Storage.State` carries an
ordered list of reserve assets, each with the Chainlink feed that prices it,
so governance can list tokenised stocks - Robinhood Chain publishes an
AggregatorV3 feed per Stock Token - and bonds in those buy real equity into
the backing. `Stringer` seeds the list with the counter token, which is the
unit of account and needs no feed; everything else is valued against it.

A listed reserve can also name the LSD pool it trades against, and the
treasury then takes whichever of the two prices is LOWER. That direction is
the whole of why a spot price is safe here without a TWAP: being paid more
LSD for a bond requires a HIGHER valuation, and a minimum cannot be pushed
up. Moving the pool against the protocol means moving it up, which the feed
then wins; moving it down only makes the treasury value the asset more
conservatively than the market does, which costs the bonder and never the
holders. The pool can tighten the answer and never loosen it, so wiring one
up is free.

The cross-check is not a substitute for the feed. It applies only when
there is a feed answer to compare against; a dead feed still closes
bonding. The comparison side is a `Spyglass` (protocol/v4): a read-only,
fully immutable adapter over the asset's own deep asset/USDG Uniswap V4
pool, reporting the spot price of one whole asset in counter units. Beyond
tightening the answer, it is the treasury's around-the-clock eye: the
stock feeds follow the market's 24/5 hours while the pools trade on, so
through a weekend the min() still has a live, conservative input. The min
only applies inside a band: past RESERVE_MAX_DIVERGENCE (10%) apart, in
either direction, one of the two sources is wrong and the treasury refuses
to price the asset at all, which closes bonding and touches nothing else.

Feeds are trusted in exactly one direction. Pricing decides how much LSD a
bond pays out, and a bond the protocol cannot price is a bond it refuses to
sell: a feed that is stale beyond the reserve staleness window, or answers
zero or negative, closes bonding. That window starts at three days - wide
enough to carry a 24/5 feed across a weekend, with the pool cross-check
covering the gap conservatively -
and is in storage rather than compiled in, because the true heartbeat is not
published and the cost of guessing wrong is asymmetric. Too tight only closes
bonding; too loose lets a bond be priced off a stale number, which dilutes
every holder. Governance moves it either way, up to a ceiling, without an
upgrade. Redemption reads no feed at all -
it pays a straight pro-rata slice of every reserve balance, which needs no
prices to be correct. A floor that only holds while an oracle is up is not a
floor.

The pro-rata argument above holds per asset, so it holds for the basket
however the prices move. Delisting an asset stops new bonds in it but leaves
the balance in the basket, because taking it out would hand it to nobody.

Listing a reserve is the one action that could be used to drain the
protocol - a worthless token paired with a lying feed bonds for real LSD -
so it is governance-only, through the `ListReserve` proposal in
`protocol/contracts/dao/Governance.sol`. There is no admin path to it.

The bond discount moved from a compile-time constant into storage, bounded
by MAX_BOND_DISCOUNT (25%) and movable only by a `treasurer` address that
`Stringer` sets to the deploying account. That address holds exactly four
powers - start the epoch clock once (`launch()`), stop new bonds instantly
(`pauseBonds()`, which only governance can undo, through the `ResumeBonds`
proposal or a fix's own initializer), move the discount, and point the gas
oracle at a feed. It cannot move funds, mint, list a reserve, or make a
bond that lowers backing, and governance can retire it by proposal. The discount decides how
attractive bonds are and nothing more: whenever the discounted price would
dip under backing it clamps there instead, so no discount setting can ever
sell below the floor or close the window.

Addition: a swap fee that feeds the backing
-------------------------------------------
`SwapFeeCollector` (new file) charges a per-hook fee on the unspecified
side of every swap - the side the pool computes rather than the one the
trader named, which is the only side a V4 `afterSwap` hook may adjust. This
required the `afterSwapReturnDelta` permission and therefore a different
mined hook address. Both exact-input and exact-output swaps are charged, so
naming an output amount is not a way around it. The oracle pool charges 5
basis points; the stock pools charge 100.

`sweep(address[])` is permissionless and moves what accumulated: LSD is
burned, everything else goes to the DAO. LSD is burned rather than forwarded
because LSD forwarded to the DAO would be credited to no one - dead weight
that raises no balance and backs nothing - whereas burning does the same
job from the other side, since fewer LSD against the same reserves is more
backing per LSD. A token that is a listed reserve is
backing the moment it arrives; one that is not sits at the DAO until
governance lists it, which is recoverable rather than lost. This is the only
part of the backing that grows without anyone choosing to bond. The split
is deliberate: the oracle pool's 5 bp stays small because a dollar-pegged
token lives on arbitrage and that pool is where the arbitrage happens; the
stock pools' 100 bp can be twenty times larger because they are not the
peg's venue - there a toll is simply revenue, and all of it is backing.

Change: the expansion limit ramps instead of standing at 10%
------------------------------------------------------------
Upstream ESD capped supply changes at SUPPLY_CHANGE_LIMIT = 10% per daily
epoch. This fork's six-hour epochs quietly turned that into ~46% a day, and
a single manipulated oracle epoch was enough to mint the full 10%. The
limit is now a throttle that climbs by half again only in epochs where
the price actually pins it, up to the unchanged 10% ceiling, and halves on
every contraction epoch down to SUPPLY_RAMP_FLOOR (2.5% - about ESD's
original daily rate at four epochs a day). It exits genesis AT the
ceiling: bootstrapping is forty-five pinned expansions, so continuing
demand is not asked to re-earn a rate it visibly holds; the unset state
field simply reads as the ceiling. A deviation below the limit expands by
the deviation and leaves the throttle alone. Debt growth in contraction
epochs is capped by the same live limit. The live value is public as
supplyLimit(); the state field is appended.

To keep this and future changes inside EIP-170, several views were
slimmed in the same stroke: backingPerDollar/bondDiscount/
effectiveBondPrice return plain uint256 instead of ABIEncoderV2 structs,
bondPrice/bondsThisEpoch/advanceIncentive went internal, the unused
gasOracle getter was dropped, and the deploy compiles with optimizer
runs=1 (deploy-size-optimal; runtime gas is a rounding error at this
chain's prices).

Change: the advance incentive is priced in gas, not in tokens
---------------------------------------------------------------
Upstream ESD paid whoever called advance() a flat 100 LSD forever. That is a
bet that the token stays cheap: the incentive is denominated in LSD and the
work it pays for is denominated in gas, so once LSD is worth something a flat
amount is wildly overpaying for one transaction.

`Greenwood.advance()` now measures the gas the call actually used,
values it through a Chainlink ETH/USD feed and the last price the Regulator
stood behind, and pays that plus ADVANCE_INCENTIVE_MARGIN percent - clamped
between ADVANCE_INCENTIVE_MIN and ADVANCE_INCENTIVE_MAX. While bootstrapping
it is flat at the maximum, because the supply is multiplying every epoch
anyway and having the epochs stepped matters more than what it costs.

The ceiling is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. The caller sets the gas
price, so without one, paying an absurd gas price and being handed 125% of it
back in LSD is a mint faucet that costs 80 cents on the dollar. The ceiling
bounds that at the amount the protocol was already willing to pay.

Every way of failing to price gas - no feed set, a stale feed, a
non-positive answer - falls through to the floor rather than reverting. A
protocol that cannot step its epoch because an oracle is down is worse than
one that underpays for a while. For the same reason the feed is set by the
treasurer rather than by vote: the window where it matters opens the moment
bootstrapping ends, sooner than a governance cycle, and the worst a wrong
feed can do is bounded by the ceiling.

This is the one change that reorders upstream's expansion path: the incentive
is minted after the three steps rather than before, because what to pay is
not known until the work is done. The caller's own incentive is therefore not
part of the supply this epoch's expansion is computed against, and lands in
the next epoch's bonded snapshot rather than the one it just took.

Addition: an LSD pair against each tokenised stock
--------------------------------------------------
Upstream ESD had one pool and minted the whole LP share of each expansion to
it. LSD runs an LSD pair against several tokenised stocks alongside the one
against the counter token, and all of them have to be worth providing to, so
`Comptroller.mintToBonded` now splits that share across a list of pools by
weight (`Comptroller.mintToPools`). This is the one place upstream's
expansion path is modified rather than added to.

Weights are relative rather than percentages, and the counter-token pool
carries the largest by default. That is not a preference: the oracle reads
its price from that pool, and if its liquidity thins out `capture()` reports
invalid and the protocol stops regulating altogether. Its depth is the
precondition for everything else, not one incentive among several.

Whatever the pools do not take - the division remainder, and the whole share
before any pool is listed - goes to bonders. An expansion must mint exactly
what the Regulator decided to mint.

The pools are plain ERC20 holders as far as the DAO is concerned: they read
their reward off their own balance, so a mint is the whole of the transfer
and nothing in the expansion path can revert on a pool's behalf. Listing one
is a governance vote (`AddPool`), but unlike listing a reserve it cannot be
used to take anything - a pool can only receive newly minted LSD during an
expansion that was already decided. The worst a bad entry does is waste
emission. `SetPoolWeight` retunes a share, and weight zero stops a pool
earning while leaving it listed so cached indices stay valid.

`TollGate` (new file) is the hook those pools share. It holds no per-pool
state - every swap arrives with its own PoolKey - so one deployment serves
every stock pair, where the oracle hook can only ever serve the one pool
whose TWAP it accumulates. Seven pairs would otherwise mean seven mined
addresses. It carries only the afterSwap and afterSwapReturnDelta bits
(0x44).

LSD is deliberately not priced against these pools. The peg is denominated
in the counter token; a price read from an LSD/AAPL pool would move with the
semiconductor cycle, and "LSD is at 1.00" would stop meaning anything. Only
the counter-token pool feeds the oracle, which is why only it has the oracle
hook.

`Quiver` is unchanged apart from exposing `sqrtPriceX96()` and
`dollarPrice()` - the latter converted in the pool rather than in the DAO
because squaring a sqrt price overflows a uint256 and needs 512-bit math,
which the 0.8 compiler has a library for and 0.5 does not. It was already
general over its PoolKey, so a stock pair needs no new pool contract. The
price is exposed raw rather than converted because the same contract runs
against a 6-decimal stablecoin and 18-decimal stocks alike, and converting
would mean baking one of those in.

Supporting changes: `Storage.State` gains the treasury's counter token, the
last trusted price, per-account bond balances, the per-epoch issuance
counters, the reserve list and its oracles, the bond discount, the treasurer,
the LP pool list with its weights and the gas oracle - all appended, so every
slot upstream ESD defined keeps its
position (the layout has to stay identical across Fletcher/Bowyer/Stringer and
Greenwood, which all run against the same proxy).
`Comptroller.balanceCheck` is `internal` rather than upstream's `private`
so Treasury can assert the same invariant, and its solvency check is `>=`
where upstream ESD used `==`. Under `==`, anyone could transfer a single
wei of LSD straight to the DAO address and every balance-changing call
would then revert on the assertion - a one-wei permanent brick. `>=` keeps
the guarantee that matters (the DAO holds at least the LSD it owes to
staged, bonded and redeemable) and tolerates a donated surplus, which is
credited to no one and reads into no other number.
`Stringer` derives the counter token by reading the LP pool's own PoolKey
rather than taking it as another hardcoded constant, so it cannot disagree
with the token the oracle prices against.

`Greenwood` compiles to about 24,230 bytes of runtime code against
EIP-170's 24,576-byte limit (optimizer runs=1), and the `ListReserve`
proposal - the largest, since every proposal inherits the whole runtime -
to 24,484, leaving roughly 90 bytes. That headroom is the real constraint
here, and it is
why several views that were public are now internal (`reserveValue`,
`bondValue`, `isPool`, `bondPrice`, `bondsThisEpoch`, `advanceIncentive`,
the removed `redeemPayouts`/`poolRewardFor`/`gasOracle`) and why
`backingPerDollar`/`bondDiscount`/`effectiveBondPrice` return plain uint256
rather than ABIEncoderV2 structs. Each dropped view is price times balance
or a weighted share, so the frontend does that arithmetic itself. Measure
before adding another public getter.

The underlying bonding/rebase/coupon/governance mechanism itself
(Bonding.sol, Market.sol, Regulator.sol, Govern.sol, Comptroller.sol,
Curve.sol, State.sol, etc.) is unmodified from ESD's original design.

